      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin stabilizes the interface by embedding its polar phosphate groups at the oil-water boundary while its hydrophobic tails anchor into the oil phase. In acidic environments, protonation of the phosphate headgroup reduces its negative charge, which eliminates electrostatic repulsion between droplets and causes them to coalesce. Additionally, the reduced charge density lowers the interfacial tension, making it energetically unfavorable to maintain the emulsion until breakdown occurs.",
